{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"display_name":"Python 3 (ipykernel)","language":"python","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.13.15","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Integrated Intelligent Systems: Tutorial 1 - Foundations (3 Pts)\n\nThis notebook introduces the fundamental components, challenges, and definitions, central to designing and implementing Cognition-Enabled Robot Agents.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Part 1: The Unified Theoretical Exercise (E1–E6) (1.5 Pt)\n\n**Title:** From Natural Language Request to action execution: The Eva Coffee Scenario\n\n![Alternative Text](coffee-task-photo.png)\n\n### Scenario Background\nEva requests: \"Bring me my coffee.\" Her usual blue mug is in the dishwasher; a white mug is available on the counter. The fill level can vary, and Eva may shift position during execution.\n\n---\n\n### Multi-Question Student Task Sheet (0.25 Pt x 6)\nStudents work through this unified exercise in the VRB or on paper, structured across the six developmental steps of Lecture 1:\n\n### 1. **E1 · Opening Judgment (Data vs. Competence):**\n\n- If an end-to-end vision-language-action model or a demonstration video shows a robot successfully serving coffee 100 times in a row, does that prove the robot \"can do the task\" across any home environment? State your reasoning and rate your confidence from 1 to 5.\n\n**Answer:**\n\n**No. Confidence: 5/5.**\n\nA robot serving coffee 100 times in a row only demonstrates reliable performance under the specific conditions of those runs, such as the same kitchen, mug, fill level, and layout. It does not prove that the robot can perform the task in any home environment.\n\nDifferent homes may introduce unfamiliar mugs, lighting conditions, obstacles, cupboard layouts, and people who move unpredictably. These variations may not have been tested during the demonstrations.\n\nTherefore, repeated success demonstrates performance on a particular distribution of conditions, rather than general competence. To establish generalization, the robot must be evaluated under different and previously untested conditions.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **E2 · Changed Case Prediction (Physical Bounds):**\n\n- A half-full white mug was carried successfully along the route. Suppose the mug is now nearly full. Predict the physical consequence during a turn, and identify the untested assumption in the original run.\n\n**Answer:**\n\n**Predicted Consequence:**\n\nWhen the mug is nearly full, there is very little free space between the coffee surface and the rim. During a turn, lateral acceleration causes the liquid to slosh, making the coffee rise toward the rim and potentially spill over.\n\n**Untested Assumption:**\n\nThe original run assumed that the half-full mug provided sufficient spill margin at the selected navigation speed and turning rate. This assumption was validated only under the half-full condition.\n\nThe safe velocity, acceleration, and turning angle were not tested with a nearly full mug. Therefore, the robot cannot assume that the same motion parameters will remain safe under the changed condition.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **E3 · The Commitment Decision (Warrant Gate):**\n\n- The blue mug is unavailable and white-mug acceptance is unconfirmed. Choose whether the robot should ask, observe, or proceed. State the single item of evidence that justifies your choice.\n\n**Answer:**\n\n**Decision: Ask.**\n\nThe robot should ask Eva whether she accepts the white mug as a substitute for her preferred blue mug and wait for her confirmation before proceeding.\n\n**Single Item of Evidence:**\n\nEva has not confirmed that she accepts the white mug, and she is available to answer the question.\n\nProceeding would rely on an unverified assumption about her preference. Observing the environment cannot reveal her personal preference either. Therefore, asking Eva is the most appropriate decision before committing to the action.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **E4 · The Five Questions of Understanding (T1–T5):**\n\n- Formulate one distinct question for each dimension—Explain ($\\text{T1}$), Predict ($\\text{T2}$), Design ($\\text{T3}$), Bound ($\\text{T4}$), and Check ($\\text{T5}$)—concerning the coffee delivery episode. At least one question must address a changed condition that a video alone cannot answer.\n\n**Answer:**\n\n**T1 (Explain):** Why did the robot choose to ask Eva about the white mug instead of simply using it?\n\n**T2 (Predict):** What will happen to the coffee if the mug is nearly full instead of half-full and the robot must make a sharp turn at the same speed as before? *(Changed condition that a video alone cannot answer.)*\n\n**T3 (Design):** How should the robot decide between asking, observing, and proceeding when the preferred mug is unavailable?\n\n**T4 (Bound):** What is the maximum fill level and turning speed at which the robot can carry the mug without spilling?\n\n**T5 (Check):** What observation after delivery confirms that the coffee arrived without spilling?\n\n---\n\n### 5. **E5 · System Decomposition (Mapping to Architecture):**\n\n- List the core functional modules required to handle Eva's request (interpreting, perceiving, proposing, predicting, warranting, acting, and recording) and map them onto the Integration Diagram.\n\n**Answer:**\n\n| Module | Role in the Eva Scenario |\n|---|---|\n| **1. Interpretation System** | Converts Eva's request, \"Bring me my coffee,\" into a formal goal and decomposes it into subgoals, such as finding a suitable mug, preparing coffee, pouring it, navigating to Eva, and handing it over. |\n| **2. Perception System** | Observes the mugs, coffee, obstacles, and Eva's position. It processes sensor information and updates the Belief State with relevant environmental information. |\n| **3. Proposer** | Generates candidate actions and plans with parameters, such as selecting the white mug, determining the coffee fill level, and choosing a navigation path and speed. |\n| **4. Prediction System (Semantic Digital Twin)** | Simulates proposed actions and predicts their potential consequences, such as liquid sloshing, coffee spilling, or the mug slipping during grasping or transportation. |\n| **5. Warrant Gate** | Evaluates whether the proposed action is feasible, safe, and repairable. It determines whether the robot should **proceed, ask, or observe**. Rejected proposals are returned for revision. |\n| **6. Execution Engine (Action System)** | Executes the approved actions using the robot's physical body, actuators, and effectors. It performs tasks such as grasping the mug, pouring coffee, navigating, and handing it over to Eva. |\n| **7. Recording / Audit Logger** | Records the original request, goals, observations, Belief States, proposals, predictions, gate decisions, execution parameters, and outcomes for evaluation, debugging, and future improvement. |\n\n**Mapping onto the Integration Diagram (Information Flow):**\n\nEva's request → **Interpretation System** (goal and subgoals) → **Perception System** (environmental observations and Belief State) → **Proposer** (candidate action generation) → **Prediction System** (consequence evaluation) → **Warrant Gate** (decision).\n\n- **Proceed:** The approved action is sent to the Execution Engine for execution.\n- **Ask:** The robot requests additional information or confirmation from Eva.\n- **Observe:** The robot gathers additional environmental information through the Perception System.\n- **Reject / Revise:** The proposal is returned to the Proposer for modification.\n\nThe Execution Engine's results are fed back into the Perception System to update the Belief State and support continuous adaptation. The Recording / Audit Logger collects relevant information from all modules, maintaining a record of the system's decisions and execution.\n\n---\n\n### 6. **E6 · Transfer & Exit Ticket (Justified Adaptation):**\n\n- Suppose Eva stands up while the robot is carrying the coffee. Revise the action, predict the potential failure mechanism, and state the exact evidence criterion required before the robot commits to the revised path.\n\n**Answer:**\n\n**1. Revised Action:**\n\nWhen Eva stands up, the robot should immediately slow down or stop to minimize liquid sloshing and avoid unsafe movement.\n\nIt should then re-evaluate the situation through the following steps:\n\n1. **Perception:** Obtain fresh sensor observations of Eva's current position, posture, and surrounding environment.\n2. **Belief State Update:** Update the robot's internal representation to reflect Eva's changed position and posture.\n3. **Proposer:** Generate a revised action proposal, including a new navigation path, velocity, and handover position.\n4. **Prediction System:** Predict the consequences of the revised action, including liquid sloshing, coffee spillage, and the possibility of dropping the mug.\n5. **Warrant Gate:** Evaluate the safety and feasibility of the revised action and approve or reject it before execution.\n\nThe robot must adapt its trajectory and velocity instead of blindly continuing with the original plan.\n\n**2. Potential Failure Mechanism:**\n\nThe primary failure mechanism is an **outdated Belief State**.\n\nThe original trajectory and handover position were calculated based on Eva's previous position and posture. If the robot continues without updating its information, the following failures may occur:\n\n- **Coffee spillage:** Sudden deceleration, excessive velocity, or sharp turns may cause liquid sloshing and spilling.\n- **Mug dropping:** The previously planned handover position may no longer be appropriate for Eva's new posture, increasing the risk of losing a secure handover.\n- **Unsafe navigation:** The original trajectory may no longer be suitable for the changed environment, potentially causing collisions or unsafe interactions.\n\nThus, even if the original prediction was correct, changes in the environment can invalidate the previous action.\n\n**3. Exact Evidence Criterion:**\n\nThe robot should commit to the revised path only when the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n1. **Fresh observation:** Sensor data confirms Eva's current position and posture.\n2. **Updated trajectory:** A revised path, navigation velocity, and handover position have been generated based on the updated Belief State.\n3. **Physical safety prediction:** The Prediction System confirms that predicted liquid sloshing remains below the allowable spill threshold for the current fill level and revised trajectory.\n4. **Secure grasp and handover:** The mug can be held securely and handed over safely under the revised conditions.\n5. **Warrant Gate approval:** The proposed action satisfies the required safety and feasibility constraints and is approved for execution.\n\n**Final Decision:** The robot commits to the revised path only when fresh observations and predictions establish that the action is safe and feasible, and the Warrant Gate approves it. Otherwise, the robot remains stopped and gathers additional information before proceeding.\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Part 2: Interactive Virtual Lab & Python Tutorial (1.5 Pt)\n\n**Title:** Implementing the Embodied Cognitive Architecture\n\n### Scenario Background\nEva requests: \"Bring me my coffee.\"\n\n---","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Embodied Cognitive Architecture\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\nfrom typing import Dict, Any, Tuple, Optional, List\nimport random\nimport time\nimport math\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass GoalSpecification:\n    \"\"\"Formalized, concrete goal specification derived via semantic interpretation.\"\"\"\n    target_object: str\n    destination: str\n    target_volume_ml: float\n    max_allowable_force_n: float\n    max_slosh_wave_mm: float\n    max_nav_velocity_mps: float\n    time_frame_seconds: float\n\n@dataclass\nclass ParameterPacket:\n    \"\"\"System 1 (The Proposer): Flexible sampler proposing execution parameters.\"\"\"\n    grasp_pose: Tuple[float, float, float]\n    max_velocity_mps: float\n    tilt_rate_radps: float\n    source_generator: str = \"stochastic_sampler\"\n    contact_depth_m: float = 0.0\n\n\nclass Proposer:\n    \"\"\"\n    System 1 (The Proposer):\n    Generates candidate execution parameters\n    using a Gaussian probabilistic model.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        mean_velocity: float = 0.15,\n        std_velocity: float = 0.05,\n        mean_tilt_rate: float = 0.10,\n        std_tilt_rate: float = 0.03\n    ):\n        self.mean_velocity = mean_velocity\n        self.std_velocity = std_velocity\n\n        self.mean_tilt_rate = mean_tilt_rate\n        self.std_tilt_rate = std_tilt_rate\n\n    def propose(self, belief: BeliefState,\n                goal: GoalSpecification) -> ParameterPacket:\n\n        # Sample candidate action parameters\n        velocity = random.gauss(\n            self.mean_velocity,\n            self.std_velocity\n        )\n\n        tilt_rate = random.gauss(\n            self.mean_tilt_rate,\n            self.std_tilt_rate\n        )\n\n        # Restrict parameters to feasible ranges\n        velocity = max(\n            0.05,\n            min(velocity, goal.max_nav_velocity_mps)\n        )\n\n        tilt_rate = max(\n            0.01,\n            min(tilt_rate, 0.25)\n        )\n\n        # Generate candidate grasp pose\n        grasp_pose = (0.45, 0.12, 0.30)\n\n        return ParameterPacket(\n            grasp_pose=grasp_pose,\n            max_velocity_mps=velocity,\n            tilt_rate_radps=tilt_rate,\n            source_generator=\"gaussian_proposer\"\n        )\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Observation:\n    \"\"\"Raw, noisy sensor measurements at time t (distinct from internal belief).\"\"\"\n    timestamp: float\n    measured_temperature_c: float\n    measured_friction: float\n    eva_position: str\n    room_busyness: str\n\n@dataclass\nclass BeliefState:\n    \"\"\"The robot's current internal belief state (updated via perception).\"\"\"\n\n    battery_level_percent: float = 80.0\n    total_battery_capacity_wh: float = 100.0\n    pouch_present: bool = True\n    pouch_material: str = \"flexible_plastic\"\n    fluid_viscosity_pas: float = 1.2\n    vial_ready: bool = True\n    camera_active: bool = True\n    fallback_path_exists: bool = True\n    audit_logger_enabled: bool = True\n    # Real-time environmental parameters maintained internally\n    ambient_temperature_c: float = 21.5\n    surface_friction_coefficient: float = 0.45\n    eva_position: str = \"living_room\"\n    room_busyness: str = \"medium\"  # 'low', 'medium', 'high'\n    preferred_cup: str = \"blue_ceramic_mug\"\n\n\nclass InterpretationSystem:\n    \"\"\"Uses a semantic knowledge base to map vague instructions and context \n    into precise physical goal parameters (GoalSpecification).\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, knowledge_base: Dict[str, Any]):\n        self.kb = knowledge_base\n\n    def interpret(self, raw_utterance: str, belief: BeliefState) -> GoalSpecification:\n        print(f\"\\n[INTERPRETATION] Analyzing instruction: '{raw_utterance}' in context of Eva (Position: {belief.eva_position}, Room Busyness: {belief.room_busyness})\")\n        \n        cup_type = belief.preferred_cup\n        cup_properties = self.kb[\"cups\"].get(cup_type, {\"max_force\": 5.0, \"max_wave\": 2.0})\n        \n        busyness_factor = self.kb[\"room_busyness_limits\"].get(belief.room_busyness, 0.5)\n        max_nav_vel = 1.2 * busyness_factor\n        \n        is_urgent = \"schnell\" in raw_utterance.lower() or \"fast\" in raw_utterance.lower()\n        time_frame = 30.0 if is_urgent else 60.0\n        target_volume = 15.0 if not is_urgent else 12.0\n        \n        goal = GoalSpecification(\n            target_object=cup_type,\n            destination=belief.eva_position,\n            target_volume_ml=target_volume,\n            max_allowable_force_n=cup_properties[\"max_force\"],\n            max_slosh_wave_mm=cup_properties[\"max_wave\"],\n            max_nav_velocity_mps=max_nav_vel,\n            time_frame_seconds=time_frame\n        )\n        \n        print(f\" -> Derived Goal: Object={goal.target_object}, MaxForce={goal.max_allowable_force_n}N, MaxNavVel={goal.max_nav_velocity_mps}m/s, TimeFrame={goal.time_frame_seconds}s\")\n        return goal\n\n\nclass SensingSystem:\n    \"\"\"Simulates real-time noisy hardware sensors emitting raw observations.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self):\n        pass\n\n    def sense(self, true_ambient_temp: float, true_friction: float, eva_pos: str, busyness: str) -> Observation:\n        noisy_temp = true_ambient_temp + random.uniform(-0.5, 0.5)\n        noisy_friction = true_friction + random.uniform(-0.05, 0.05)\n        \n        return Observation(\n            timestamp=time.time(),\n            measured_temperature_c=round(noisy_temp, 2),\n            measured_friction=round(noisy_friction, 2),\n            eva_position=eva_pos,\n            room_busyness=busyness\n        )\n\n\nclass PerceptionSystem:\n    \"\"\"Estimator module that updates the internal BeliefState using raw Observations.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, belief_state: BeliefState):\n        self.belief_state = belief_state\n\n    def update(self, observation: Observation) -> None:\n        self.belief_state.ambient_temperature_c = observation.measured_temperature_c\n        self.belief_state.surface_friction_coefficient = observation.measured_friction\n        self.belief_state.eva_position = observation.eva_position\n        self.belief_state.room_busyness = observation.room_busyness\n\n\n\nclass SemanticDigitalTwin:\n    \"\"\"System 2 (Semantic Digital Twin): Imperfect models with sim-to-real epistemic gaps.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, belief_state: BeliefState):\n        self.belief = belief_state\n\n    def predict_properties(\n        self,\n        params: ParameterPacket,\n        goal: GoalSpecification\n    ) -> Dict[str, float]:\n\n        # Stage 1: Initial compliance of the flexible pouch\n        stiffness_coefficient = 12.5 * (\n            1.0 + self.belief.surface_friction_coefficient * 0.1\n        )\n\n        # Stage 2: Bottoming-out effect against the rigid fixture\n        contact_depth_m = getattr(params, \"contact_depth_m\", 0.0)\n\n        if contact_depth_m > 0.02:\n            stiffness_coefficient *= 1.8\n\n        # Non-linear power-law contact force model\n        predicted_force = (\n            stiffness_coefficient *\n            (params.max_velocity_mps ** 1.5)\n        )\n\n        # Temperature-dependent fluid viscosity\n        effective_viscosity = (\n            self.belief.fluid_viscosity_pas *\n            (1.0 - (self.belief.ambient_temperature_c - 20.0) * 0.01)\n        )\n\n        # Fluid sloshing model\n        radius = 0.05\n\n        wave_height_m = (\n            params.tilt_rate_radps * radius\n        ) / effective_viscosity\n\n        predicted_wave = wave_height_m * 1000.0\n\n        # Sim-to-real epistemic uncertainty\n        epistemic_force_error = random.uniform(-0.2, 0.3)\n        epistemic_wave_error = random.uniform(-0.1, 0.2)\n\n        return {\n            \"predicted_force_n\": round(\n                max(0.0, predicted_force + epistemic_force_error), 2\n            ),\n            \"predicted_wave_mm\": round(\n                max(0.0, predicted_wave + epistemic_wave_error), 2\n            ),\n            \"predicted_nav_velocity\": min(\n                params.max_velocity_mps,\n                goal.max_nav_velocity_mps\n            )\n        }\n\n\n\n\nclass WarrantGate:\n    \"\"\"Validates predicted properties against the interpreted GoalSpecification.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, sdt: SemanticDigitalTwin):\n        self.sdt = sdt\n\n    def validate(self, params: ParameterPacket, goal: GoalSpecification, belief: BeliefState) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        predictions = self.sdt.predict_properties(params, goal)\n                # === Q4.1: Energetic Viability Check ===\n        estimated_energy_drain_wh = (\n            2.0 + 0.15 * (8.0 / max(params.max_velocity_mps, 0.001))\n        )\n\n        available_energy_wh = (\n            belief.total_battery_capacity_wh\n            * belief.battery_level_percent / 100.0\n        )\n\n        energetically_viable = (\n            estimated_energy_drain_wh\n            < 0.15 * belief.total_battery_capacity_wh\n            and estimated_energy_drain_wh <= available_energy_wh\n        )\n        \n        checks = {\n            \"grounded\": belief.vial_ready and belief.pouch_present,\n            \"feasible\": params.max_velocity_mps > 0.0 and params.tilt_rate_radps > 0.0,\n            \"safe\": (predictions[\"predicted_force_n\"] <= goal.max_allowable_force_n) and \n                    (predictions[\"predicted_wave_mm\"] <= goal.max_slosh_wave_mm) and\n                    (predictions[\"predicted_nav_velocity\"] <= goal.max_nav_velocity_mps),\n            \"monitorable\": belief.camera_active,\n            \"repairable\": belief.fallback_path_exists,\n            \"recordable\": belief.audit_logger_enabled,\n\n            # TASK 4.1: Energy viability check\n             \"energetically_viable\": energetically_viable,\n        }\n        \n        all_passed = all(checks.values())\n        failed_criterion = next((name for name, passed in checks.items() if not passed), None)\n        \n        return {\n            \"passed\": all_passed,\n            \"failed_criterion\": failed_criterion,\n            \"predictions\": predictions,\n            \"warrant_checks\": checks\n        }\n\n\n\nclass ExecutionEngine:\n    \"\"\"Manages physical execution and constructs the epistemic audit trail.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.audit_trail: List[Dict[str, Any]] = []\n\n    def execute_action(self, params: ParameterPacket, goal: GoalSpecification, warrant_result: Dict[str, Any]) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        print(f\"\\n[ACTUATOR] Executing motion in physical world: v_max={params.max_velocity_mps} m/s\")\n        time.sleep(0.1)\n        \n        # Real-world divergence check\n        predictions = warrant_result[\"predictions\"]\n        actual_force = predictions[\"predicted_force_n\"] + random.uniform(-0.3, 0.6)\n        actual_wave = predictions[\"predicted_wave_mm\"] + random.uniform(-0.2, 0.5)\n        \n        spill_occurred = (actual_force > goal.max_allowable_force_n) or (actual_wave > goal.max_slosh_wave_mm)\n        status = \"FAILURE\" if spill_occurred else \"SUCCESS\"\n        \n        execution_outcome = {\n            \"actual_force_n\": round(actual_force, 2),\n            \"actual_wave_mm\": round(actual_wave, 2),\n            \"actual_volume_transferred_ml\": goal.target_volume_ml if not spill_occurred else goal.target_volume_ml - 3.0,\n            \"spill_occurred\": spill_occurred,\n            \"status\": status\n        }\n        return execution_outcome\n\n    def record_trace(self, asked_utterance: str, goal: GoalSpecification, belief: BeliefState, \n                     proposal: ParameterPacket, warrant_result: Dict[str, Any], \n                     observation: Observation, committed: bool, execution_outcome: Optional[Dict[str, Any]] = None) -> None:\n        record = {\n            \"timestamp\": time.time(),\n            \"asked\": {\n                \"raw_utterance\": asked_utterance,\n                \"interpreter_output\": {\n                    \"target\": goal.target_object,\n                    \"max_force_limit_n\": goal.max_allowable_force_n,\n                    \"max_nav_velocity\": goal.max_nav_velocity_mps\n                }\n            },\n            \"belief\": {\n                \"eva_position\": belief.eva_position,\n                \"room_busyness\": belief.room_busyness,\n                \"ambient_temp_c\": belief.ambient_temperature_c,\n                \"surface_friction\": belief.surface_friction_coefficient\n            },\n            \"observed\": {\n                \"timestamp\": observation.timestamp,\n                \"measured_temp_c\": observation.measured_temperature_c,\n                \"measured_friction\": observation.measured_friction,\n                \"eva_position\": observation.eva_position,\n                \"room_busyness\": observation.room_busyness\n            },\n            \"committed\": {\n                \"action_approved\": committed,\n                \"proposed_parameters\": {\n                    \"max_velocity_mps\": proposal.max_velocity_mps,\n                    \"tilt_rate_radps\": proposal.tilt_rate_radps\n                },\n                \"warrant_verdict\": warrant_result\n            },\n            \"execution_outcome\": execution_outcome\n        }\n        self.audit_trail.append(record)\n\n        # TASK 5.1: Post-Hoc Epistemic Audit Analyzer\n\n\n\ndef audit_analyzer(audit_trail):\n    total_rejected = 0\n    total_approved = 0\n\n    rejection_reasons = {}\n    force_errors = []\n\n    for record in audit_trail:\n\n        committed = record.get(\"committed\", {})\n        warrant = committed.get(\"warrant_verdict\", {})\n\n        outcome = record.get(\"execution_outcome\")\n\n        # Count rejected proposals\n        if not committed.get(\"action_approved\", False):\n            total_rejected += 1\n\n            failed_criterion = warrant.get(\"failed_criterion\")\n\n            if failed_criterion:\n                rejection_reasons[failed_criterion] = (\n                    rejection_reasons.get(failed_criterion, 0) + 1\n                )\n\n        # Count approved physical executions\n        elif outcome is not None:\n            total_approved += 1\n\n        # Calculate predicted vs actual force error\n        predictions = warrant.get(\"predictions\", {})\n\n        if outcome is not None:\n            predicted_force = predictions.get(\"predicted_force_n\")\n            actual_force = outcome.get(\"actual_force_n\")\n\n            if predicted_force is not None and actual_force is not None:\n                error = abs(actual_force - predicted_force)\n                force_errors.append(error)\n\n    # Find most frequent rejection criterion\n    if rejection_reasons:\n        most_frequent_criterion = max(\n            rejection_reasons,\n            key=rejection_reasons.get\n        )\n        rejection_count = rejection_reasons[most_frequent_criterion]\n    else:\n        most_frequent_criterion = \"None\"\n        rejection_count = 0\n\n    # Calculate average absolute force error\n    if force_errors:\n        average_force_error = sum(force_errors) / len(force_errors)\n    else:\n        average_force_error = 0.0\n\n    # Display audit analysis\n    print(\"\\n========== TASK 5.1: AUDIT ANALYSIS ==========\")\n\n    print(f\"Total Rejected Proposals : {total_rejected}\")\n    print(f\"Total Approved Executions: {total_approved}\")\n\n    print(\n        f\"Most Frequent Rejection Criterion: \"\n        f\"{most_frequent_criterion} ({rejection_count} times)\"\n    )\n\n    print(\n        f\"Average Absolute Force Error: \"\n        f\"{average_force_error:.3f} N\"\n    )\n\n    print(\"============================================\")\n\n\n\n# --- Execution Demonstration ---\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    knowledge_base = {\n        \"cups\": {\n            \"blue_ceramic_mug\": {\"max_force\": 4.5, \"max_wave\": 1.5},\n            \"plastic_cup\": {\"max_force\": 7.0, \"max_wave\": 3.0},\n            \"white_mug\": {\"max_force\": 4.5, \"max_wave\": 1.5},  # Provisional limits\n        },\n        \"room_busyness_limits\": {\n            \"low\": 1.0,\n            \"medium\": 0.6,\n            \"high\": 0.3\n        }\n    }\n\n    # 1. Initialize systems\n    belief_state = BeliefState(\n        battery_level_percent=80.0,\n        room_busyness=\"medium\",\n        preferred_cup=\"white_mug\"\n    )\n    sensing = SensingSystem()\n    perception = PerceptionSystem(belief_state)\n    interpreter = InterpretationSystem(knowledge_base)\n    sdt = SemanticDigitalTwin(belief_state)\n    warrant_gate = WarrantGate(sdt)\n    executive = ExecutionEngine()\n    \n    print(\"=== STEP 1: Sensing & Perception Update ===\")\n    raw_observation = sensing.sense(\n        true_ambient_temp=21.5, \n        true_friction=0.45, \n        eva_pos=\"living_room\", \n        busyness=\"medium\"\n    )\n    perception.update(raw_observation)\n    print(f\"Raw Sensor Observation : {raw_observation}\")\n    print(f\"Updated Internal Belief : Temp={belief_state.ambient_temperature_c}°C, Friction={belief_state.surface_friction_coefficient}\")\n    \n    print(\"\\n=== STEP 2: Instruction Interpretation ===\")\n    raw_command = \"Bring me my coffee fast!\"\n    goal = interpreter.interpret(raw_command, belief_state)\n        # === TASK 2.2: AGGRESSIVE APPROACH VELOCITY TEST ===\n\n    aggressive_proposal = ParameterPacket(\n        grasp_pose=(0.45, 0.12, 0.30),\n        max_velocity_mps=0.35,\n        tilt_rate_radps=0.08,\n        source_generator=\"aggressive_test\",\n        contact_depth_m=0.025\n    )\n\n    # Predict physical properties without random noise\n    original_uniform = random.uniform\n    random.uniform = lambda a, b: 0.0\n\n    predictions = sdt.predict_properties(\n        aggressive_proposal,\n        goal\n    )\n\n    random.uniform = original_uniform\n\n    warrant_result = warrant_gate.validate(\n        aggressive_proposal,\n        goal,\n        belief_state\n    )\n\n    print(\"\\n=== TASK 2.2: AGGRESSIVE VELOCITY TEST ===\")\n    print(f\"Approach Velocity: {aggressive_proposal.max_velocity_mps} m/s\")\n    print(f\"Contact Depth: {aggressive_proposal.contact_depth_m} m\")\n    print(f\"Predicted Force: {predictions['predicted_force_n']} N\")\n    print(f\"Allowed Force: {goal.max_allowable_force_n} N\")\n    print(f\"Warrant Checks: {warrant_result['warrant_checks']}\")\n    print(f\"Gate Passed: {warrant_result['passed']}\")\n    print(f\"Failed Criterion: {warrant_result['failed_criterion']}\")\n\n    assert predictions[\"predicted_force_n\"] > goal.max_allowable_force_n\n    assert warrant_result[\"passed\"] is False\n    assert warrant_result[\"failed_criterion\"] == \"safe\"\n\n    print(\"\\nTEST PASSED: Warrant Gate rejected the unsafe proposal.\")\n    print(\"Physical execution was prevented.\")\n\n\n    # === TASK 3.2: HIGH-TEMPERATURE FLUID SLOSHING TEST ===\n\n    print(\"\\n=== TASK 3.2: HIGH-TEMPERATURE TEST ===\")\n\n    # Temporarily change ambient temperature to 28°C\n    original_temperature = belief_state.ambient_temperature_c\n    belief_state.ambient_temperature_c = 28.0\n\n    # Create a proposal with the required tilt rate\n    high_temperature_proposal = ParameterPacket(\n        grasp_pose=(0.45, 0.12, 0.30),\n        max_velocity_mps=0.20,\n        tilt_rate_radps=0.15\n    )\n\n    # Disable random noise for reproducible predictions\n    original_uniform = random.uniform\n    random.uniform = lambda a, b: 0.0\n\n    high_temp_predictions = sdt.predict_properties(\n        high_temperature_proposal, goal\n    )\n\n    random.uniform = original_uniform\n\n    # Validate using the Warrant Gate\n    high_temp_warrant = warrant_gate.validate(\n        high_temperature_proposal, goal, belief_state\n    )\n\n    print(f\"Ambient Temperature: {belief_state.ambient_temperature_c}°C\")\n\n    \n    effective_viscosity = (\n    belief_state.fluid_viscosity_pas\n    * (1 - (28.0 - 20.0) * 0.01)\n   )\n\n\n\n    print(f\"Effective Viscosity: {effective_viscosity:.3f} Pa·s\")\n    print(f\"Tilt Rate: {high_temperature_proposal.tilt_rate_radps} rad/s\")\n    print(f\"Predicted Wave Amplitude: {high_temp_predictions['predicted_wave_mm']:.2f} mm\")\n    \n    print(f\"Maximum Allowed Wave: {goal.max_slosh_wave_mm} mm\")\n\n    print(f\"Warrant Gate Passed: {high_temp_warrant['passed']}\")\n    print(f\"Failed Criterion: {high_temp_warrant['failed_criterion']}\")\n\n    # Restore original temperature\n    belief_state.ambient_temperature_c = original_temperature\n\n    \n    print(\"\\n=== STEP 3: System 1 Proposer Generates Parameters ===\")\n    proposal_v1 = ParameterPacket(\n        grasp_pose=(0.45, 0.12, 0.30),\n        max_velocity_mps=0.8, \n        tilt_rate_radps=0.2\n    )\n    print(f\"Proposer suggests: v_max = {proposal_v1.max_velocity_mps} m/s\")\n    \n    print(\"\\n=== STEP 4: System 2 SDT & Warrant Gate Validation (Attempt 1) ===\")\n    warrant_v1 = warrant_gate.validate(proposal_v1, goal, belief_state)\n    print(f\"SDT Predictions: Nav-Vel = {warrant_v1['predictions']['predicted_nav_velocity']} m/s\")\n    print(f\"Warrant Checks  : {warrant_v1['warrant_checks']}\")\n    print(f\"Gate Verdict    : REJECTED (Failed on: {warrant_v1['failed_criterion']})\")\n        # === TASK 4.2: LOW BATTERY RECHARGE TEST ===\n\nbelief_state.battery_level_percent = 2.0\n\nprint(\"\\n=== TASK 4.2: LOW BATTERY TEST ===\")\n\nproposal_low_battery = ParameterPacket(\n    grasp_pose=(0.45, 0.12, 0.30),\n    max_velocity_mps=0.45,\n    tilt_rate_radps=0.03\n)\n\nwarrant_low_battery = warrant_gate.validate(\n    proposal_low_battery,\n    goal,\n    belief_state\n)\n\nprint(\"Warrant Checks:\",\n      warrant_low_battery[\"warrant_checks\"])\n\nprint(\"Gate Passed:\",\n      warrant_low_battery[\"passed\"])\n\nprint(\"Failed Criterion:\",\n      warrant_low_battery[\"failed_criterion\"])\n\nif not warrant_low_battery[\"warrant_checks\"][\"energetically_viable\"]:\n    print(\"\\n[LOW BATTERY] Insufficient energy.\")\n    print(\"[WORKFLOW] Coffee delivery rejected.\")\n    print(\"[ACTION] RECHARGE_REQUESTED\")\n\n# Restore battery for the remaining demonstration\n    belief_state.battery_level_percent = 80.0\n    \n    executive.record_trace(raw_command, goal, belief_state, proposal_v1, warrant_v1, raw_observation, committed=False)\n    \n    print(\"\\n=== STEP 5: Proposer Resamples (Attempt 2) ===\")\n    proposal_v2 = ParameterPacket(\n        grasp_pose=(0.45, 0.12, 0.30),\n        max_velocity_mps=0.45, \n        tilt_rate_radps=0.025\n    )\n    \n    warrant_v2 = warrant_gate.validate(proposal_v2, goal, belief_state)\n    print(f\"SDT Predictions: Force = {warrant_v2['predictions']['predicted_force_n']} N, Wave = {warrant_v2['predictions']['predicted_wave_mm']} mm\")\n    print(f\"Warrant Checks  : {warrant_v2['warrant_checks']}\")\n    verdict = \"APPROVED\" if warrant_v2[\"passed\"] else \"REJECTED\"\n    print(f\"Gate Verdict    : {verdict}\")\n    \n    print(\"\\n=== STEP 6: Physical Execution & Epistemic Audit Trail ===\")\n    if warrant_v2[\"passed\"]:\n        final_outcome = executive.execute_action(proposal_v2, goal, warrant_v2)\n        executive.record_trace(raw_command, goal, belief_state, proposal_v2, warrant_v2, raw_observation, committed=True, execution_outcome=final_outcome)\n        print(f\"Execution Outcome: {final_outcome}\")\n\n    print(f\"\\n--- COMPLETE EPISODIC AUDIT TRAIL ({len(executive.audit_trail)} Entries) ---\")\n    for idx, trace in enumerate(executive.audit_trail, 1):\n        status = trace['committed']['action_approved']\n        outcome = trace.get('execution_outcome')\n        outcome_str = f\" | Outcome: {outcome['status']} (Spill: {outcome['spill_occurred']})\" if outcome else \"\"\n        print(f\"Entry #{idx} | Approved: {status}{outcome_str} | Command: '{trace['asked']['raw_utterance']}'\")\n\n        print(\"\\n--- COMPLETE EPISODIC AUDIT TRAIL ---\")\n\nprint(\"\\n=== TASK 5.1: RUNNING AUDIT ANALYZER ===\")\naudit_analyzer(executive.audit_trail)\n","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"=== STEP 1: Sensing & Perception Update ===\nRaw Sensor Observation : Observation(timestamp=1793688020.7005599, measured_temperature_c=21.16, measured_friction=0.46, eva_position='living_room', room_busyness='medium')\nUpdated Internal Belief : Temp=21.16°C, Friction=0.46\n\n=== STEP 2: Instruction Interpretation ===\n\n[INTERPRETATION] Analyzing instruction: 'Bring me my coffee fast!' in context of Eva (Position: living_room, Room Busyness: medium)\n -> Derived Goal: Object=white_mug, MaxForce=4.5N, MaxNavVel=0.72m/s, TimeFrame=30.0s\n\n=== TASK 2.2: AGGRESSIVE VELOCITY TEST ===\nApproach Velocity: 0.35 m/s\nContact Depth: 0.025 m\nPredicted Force: 4.87 N\nAllowed Force: 4.5 N\nWarrant Checks: {'grounded': True, 'feasible': True, 'safe': False, 'monitorable': True, 'repairable': True, 'recordable': True, 'energetically_viable': True}\nGate Passed: False\nFailed Criterion: safe\n\nTEST PASSED: Warrant Gate rejected the unsafe proposal.\nPhysical execution was prevented.\n\n=== TASK 3.2: HIGH-TEMPERATURE TEST ===\nAmbient Temperature: 28.0°C\nEffective Viscosity: 1.104 Pa·s\nTilt Rate: 0.15 rad/s\nPredicted Wave Amplitude: 6.79 mm\nMaximum Allowed Wave: 1.5 mm\nWarrant Gate Passed: False\nFailed Criterion: safe\n\n=== STEP 3: System 1 Proposer Generates Parameters ===\nProposer suggests: v_max = 0.8 m/s\n\n=== STEP 4: System 2 SDT & Warrant Gate Validation (Attempt 1) ===\nSDT Predictions: Nav-Vel = 0.72 m/s\nWarrant Checks  : {'grounded': True, 'feasible': True, 'safe': False, 'monitorable': True, 'repairable': True, 'recordable': True, 'energetically_viable': True}\nGate Verdict    : REJECTED (Failed on: safe)\n\n=== TASK 4.2: LOW BATTERY TEST ===\nWarrant Checks: {'grounded': True, 'feasible': True, 'safe': True, 'monitorable': True, 'repairable': True, 'recordable': True, 'energetically_viable': False}\nGate Passed: False\nFailed Criterion: energetically_viable\n\n[LOW BATTERY] Insufficient energy.\n[WORKFLOW] Coffee delivery rejected.\n[ACTION] RECHARGE_REQUESTED\n\n=== STEP 5: Proposer Resamples (Attempt 2) ===\nSDT Predictions: Force = 3.85 N, Wave = 1.09 mm\nWarrant Checks  : {'grounded': True, 'feasible': True, 'safe': True, 'monitorable': True, 'repairable': True, 'recordable': True, 'energetically_viable': True}\nGate Verdict    : APPROVED\n\n=== STEP 6: Physical Execution & Epistemic Audit Trail ===\n\n[ACTUATOR] Executing motion in physical world: v_max=0.45 m/s\nExecution Outcome: {'actual_force_n': 3.63, 'actual_wave_mm': 1.17, 'actual_volume_transferred_ml': 12.0, 'spill_occurred': False, 'status': 'SUCCESS'}\n\n--- COMPLETE EPISODIC AUDIT TRAIL (2 Entries) ---\nEntry #1 | Approved: False | Command: 'Bring me my coffee fast!'\n\n--- COMPLETE EPISODIC AUDIT TRAIL ---\nEntry #2 | Approved: True | Outcome: SUCCESS (Spill: False) | Command: 'Bring me my coffee fast!'\n\n--- COMPLETE EPISODIC AUDIT TRAIL ---\n\n=== TASK 5.1: RUNNING AUDIT ANALYZER ===\n\n========== TASK 5.1: AUDIT ANALYSIS ==========\nTotal Rejected Proposals : 1\nTotal Approved Executions: 1\nMost Frequent Rejection Criterion: safe (1 times)\nAverage Absolute Force Error: 0.220 N\n============================================\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":11},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Questions (0.25 Pt x 4 + 0.5 Pt)\n\n### Task 1: Architectural Mapping & Code Decomposition\n* **Question 1.1:** Map the classes and methods implemented in your Python script directly onto the functional blocks of the lecture integration diagram. Explain how data flows between these components during a single cognitive decision cycle.\n* \n### Mapping of Functional Blocks to Python Classes\n\n| Functional Block | Python Class / Method | Description |\n|---|---|---|\n| Goal Specification | `GoalSpecification` | Represents Eva's natural-language request as a structured goal. |\n| Perception | `SensingSystem.sense()` and `PerceptionSystem.update()` | Collects sensor observations and updates environmental information. |\n| Belief State | `BeliefState` | Maintains the robot's internal representation of the environment, battery level, fluid properties, and other relevant information. |\n| Action Proposer | `Proposer.propose()` | Generates candidate action parameters using a Gaussian probabilistic model. |\n| Semantic Digital Twin | `SemanticDigitalTwin.predict_properties()` | Predicts contact force, fluid wave height, and navigation velocity. |\n| Warrant Gate | `WarrantGate.validate()` | Evaluates the proposed action against seven criteria: grounded, feasible, safe, monitorable, repairable, recordable, and energetically viable. |\n| Action Execution | `ExecutionEngine.execute_action()` | Executes an approved action and returns the physical execution outcome. |\n| Monitoring and Observation | `SensingSystem.sense()` and `ExecutionEngine.execute_action()` | Provides environmental observations and execution outcome measurements for evaluating actual results. |\n| Episodic Memory / Audit | `ExecutionEngine.record_trace()` and `ExecutionEngine.audit_trail` | Records the request, belief state, proposed parameters, warrant decision, and execution outcome. |\n| Cognitive Decision Cycle | Main execution workflow | Coordinates perception, interpretation, proposal, prediction, validation, execution, and audit recording. |\n\n### Data Flow During a Single Cognitive Decision Cycle\n\n1. **Goal formulation:** The `InterpretationSystem.interpret()` method converts Eva's natural-language request into a structured `GoalSpecification`.\n2. **Perception:** `SensingSystem.sense()` gathers environmental observations, including temperature, friction, Eva's position, and room busyness.\n3. **Belief update:** `PerceptionSystem.update()` incorporates the sensor observations into the `BeliefState`.\n4. **Action proposal:** `Proposer.propose()` generates candidate action parameters in a `ParameterPacket` using the current belief and goal.\n5. **Prediction:** `SemanticDigitalTwin.predict_properties()` estimates the physical consequences of the proposed action, including contact force and fluid wave height.\n6. **Safety verification:** `WarrantGate.validate()` evaluates the proposed action against seven operational criteria.\n7. **Decision and execution:** If all criteria pass, `ExecutionEngine.execute_action()` performs the action. Otherwise, the proposal is rejected before physical execution.\n8. **Observation and audit:** `ExecutionEngine.record_trace()` records the request, belief, committed decision, and execution outcome in the episodic audit trail.\n\n### Cognitive Decision Cycle\n\nThe architecture implements a closed-loop cognitive decision cycle in which the system interprets instructions, updates its belief, proposes actions, predicts their consequences, validates them against safety criteria, and executes only approved actions. Actual execution outcomes are recorded to support post-hoc auditing and analysis.\n* **Question 1.2:** Complete the proposer class based on a probabilistic model of your choice (e.g., uniform, gaussian, mixture). Justify your choice.\n* \n### Justification for Choosing the Gaussian Model\n\nI selected the Gaussian probabilistic model because robot action parameters, such as maximum velocity and tilt rate, are continuous-valued variables.\n\nThe Gaussian distribution generates candidate parameters around a predefined mean while allowing controlled variation through its standard deviation.\n\nThe reasons for choosing this model are:\n\n1. **Continuous action space:** Gaussian distributions are suitable for generating continuous robot action parameters.\n2. **Controlled exploration:** The mean represents the preferred action, while the standard deviation controls exploration.\n3. **Uncertainty representation:** Random sampling allows the proposer to account for uncertainty in selecting appropriate action parameters.\n4. **Constraint handling:** The generated parameters are restricted to feasible operational limits using clipping.\n5. **Adaptability:** The mean and standard deviation can be updated using execution feedback or reinforcement learning.\n\nTherefore, the Gaussian proposer provides a computationally efficient and flexible mechanism for generating candidate actions while maintaining controlled exploration.\n\n  \n\n### Task 2: Non-Linear Contact Mechanics and Material Compliance\nIn the base script, the contact force on the flexible pouch is modeled using a simplified power-law equation. \n* **Question 2.1:** Modify the `SemanticDigitalTwin.predict_properties` method to account for a dual-stage material compliance curve. If the contact depth exceeds $0.02 \\, \\text{m}$, the stiffness coefficient should increase exponentially by a factor of $1.8$ due to bottoming out against a rigid internal fixture.\n* Statement for Task 2.1:\n\nI modified the `SemanticDigitalTwin.predict_properties()` method in the original Python code to implement the dual-stage material compliance model. When the contact depth exceeds 0.02 m, the stiffness coefficient increases exponentially by a factor of 1.8 to account for bottoming out against a rigid internal fixture.\n\n* **Question 2.2:** Write a test case where the Proposer generates an aggressive approach velocity ($0.35 \\, \\text{m/s}$). Calculate the predicted force and verify that the Warrant Gate successfully intercepts and rejects the parameter packet before physical execution.\n* \n### Calculation of Predicted Contact Force\n\nThe contact force is modeled using a non-linear power-law equation:\n\n$$\nF = k v^{1.5}\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n- $F$ = predicted contact force (N)\n- $k$ = stiffness coefficient\n- $v$ = approach velocity (m/s)\n\nThe initial stiffness coefficient is:\n\n$$\nk_1 = 12.5(1+0.1\\mu)\n$$\n\nGiven the surface friction coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\mu = 0.45\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk_1 = 12.5(1+0.1 \\times 0.45)\n$$\n\n$$\nk_1 = 13.0625\n$$\n\nSince the contact depth exceeds $0.02\\,\\text{m}$, the material enters the second compliance stage. The stiffness increases by a factor of $1.8$:\n\n$$\nk_2 = 1.8k_1\n$$\n\n$$\nk_2 = 1.8 \\times 13.0625 = 23.5125\n$$\n\nFor the aggressive approach velocity:\n\n$$\nv = 0.35\\,\\text{m/s}\n$$\n\nThe predicted contact force becomes:\n\n$$\nF = 23.5125 \\times (0.35)^{1.5}\n$$\n\n$$\nF \\approx 23.5125 \\times 0.2071\n$$\n\n$$\n\\boxed{F \\approx 4.87\\,\\text{N}}\n$$\n\nThe allowable force for the blue ceramic mug is:\n\n$$\nF_{\\max} = 4.5\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nSince:\n\n$$\n4.87\\,\\text{N} > 4.5\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nthe proposed action violates the safety constraint.\n\n### Warrant Gate Verification\n\nThe Warrant Gate evaluates the predicted force against the maximum allowable force specified in the `GoalSpecification`.\n\nThe `safe` criterion evaluates to `False` because the predicted contact force exceeds the permissible threshold.\n\nConsequently:\n\n- The proposal is rejected by the Warrant Gate.\n- The failed criterion is `safe`.\n- The `RobotExecutor` is not invoked.\n- The unsafe action is intercepted before physical execution.\n\n### Conclusion\n\nThe dual-stage compliance model captures the increased stiffness that occurs when a flexible pouch bottoms out against a rigid internal fixture. The test demonstrates that the Semantic Digital Twin predicts a contact force of approximately $4.87\\,\\text{N}$ for the aggressive approach velocity of $0.35\\,\\text{m/s}$.\n\nBecause this exceeds the allowable force of $4.5\\,\\text{N}$, the Warrant Gate successfully rejects the proposal, preventing potentially damaging physical interaction.\n\n\n### Task 3: Advanced Fluid Sloshing and Viscosity Temperature Coupling\nFluids in laboratory automation often exhibit temperature-dependent viscosity changes.\n* **Question 3.1:** Enhance the fluid dynamics model so that ambient temperature fluctuations directly alter the effective fluid viscosity using an Arrhenius-type approximation or a linear thermal degradation coefficient ($1\\% \\, \\text{drop per} \\, ^\\circ\\text{C}$ above $20^\\circ\\text{C}$).\n* I modified the `SemanticDigitalTwin.predict_properties()` method to incorporate temperature-dependent fluid viscosity using a linear thermal degradation coefficient. The effective viscosity decreases as ambient temperature increases, directly influencing the predicted fluid sloshing and wave height. This enables the Semantic Digital Twin to account for environmental temperature variations during robotic manipulation.\n* **Question 3.2:** Simulate an environment where the ambient temperature rises to $28^\\circ\\text{C}$. How does this change the predicted wave height for a tilt rate of $0.15 \\, \\text{rad/s}$? Does the Warrant Gate trigger a safety violation?\n* \n\n### 1. Calculation of Effective Fluid Viscosity\n\nThe temperature-dependent viscosity is calculated using the linear thermal degradation model implemented in Task 3.1:\n\n$$\n\\mu(T)=\\mu_{20}[1-0.01(T-20)]\n$$\n\nGiven:\n\n$$\n\\mu_{20}=1.2\\,\\text{Pa·s}, \\quad T=28^\\circ\\text{C}\n$$\n\nSubstituting:\n\n$$\n\\mu(28)=1.2[1-0.01(28-20)]\n$$\n\n$$\n=1.2(0.92)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\mu(28)=1.104\\,\\text{Pa·s}}\n$$\n\n### 2. Calculation of Predicted Wave Height\n\nThe fluid sloshing model is given by:\n\n$$\nH=\\frac{\\omega r}{\\mu(T)}\\times1000\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n- $H$ = predicted wave height (mm)\n- $\\omega$ = tilt rate (rad/s)\n- $r$ = container radius (m)\n- $\\mu(T)$ = effective fluid viscosity (Pa·s)\n\nSubstituting the values:\n\n$$\nH=\\frac{0.15\\times0.05}{1.104}\\times1000\n$$\n\n$$\n\\boxed{H\\approx6.79\\,\\text{mm}}\n$$\n\n### 3. Warrant Gate Safety Verification\n\nThe maximum allowable wave height for the blue ceramic mug is:\n\n$$\nH_{\\max}=1.5\\,\\text{mm}\n$$\n\nComparing the predicted wave height with the allowable limit:\n\n$$\n6.79\\,\\text{mm}>1.5\\,\\text{mm}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the predicted wave height exceeds the safety threshold.\n\nThe Warrant Gate evaluates the `safe` criterion as `False`, resulting in rejection of the proposed action before physical execution.\n\n### Conclusion\n\nWhen the ambient temperature increases to $28^\\circ\\text{C}$, the effective fluid viscosity decreases to $1.104\\,\\text{Pa·s}$. Consequently, the predicted wave height increases to approximately $6.79\\,\\text{mm}$ for a tilt rate of $0.15\\,\\text{rad/s}$.\n\nSince the predicted wave height exceeds the maximum allowable limit of $1.5\\,\\text{mm}$, the Warrant Gate triggers a safety violation and rejects the action before physical execution. This demonstrates the importance of incorporating temperature-dependent viscosity into the fluid dynamics model for safe robotic manipulation.\n\n### Task 4: Expanding the Warrant Gate Checklist\nThe current Warrant Gate evaluates six core criteria (*grounded, feasible, safe, monitorable, repairable, recordable*). \n* **Question 4.1:** Add a seventh criterion called **`energetically_viable`** to the Warrant Gate dictionary checks. It must evaluate whether the estimated battery drain for the requested trajectory stays below $15\\%$ of total capacity.\n\nI extended the `WarrantGate.validate()` method by adding a seventh criterion called `energetically_viable`.\n\nThe estimated energy drain for the proposed trajectory is calculated using the action's velocity. The criterion checks whether the estimated energy consumption is below 15% of the robot's total battery capacity and whether sufficient energy is currently available in the battery.\n\nThe criterion is implemented as:\n\n`energetically_viable = estimated_energy_drain_wh < 0.15 * total_battery_capacity_wh`\n\nThis additional condition ensures that the robot does not commit to an action that exceeds its energy budget. The Warrant Gate rejects proposals that are energetically infeasible, even if they satisfy the other safety and operational criteria.\n* **Question 4.2:** Introduce a low-battery state into the `BeliefState` and demonstrate how a failed `energetically_viable` check redirects the system workflow to request a recharge action rather than proceeding with the coffee delivery.\n\n\nI introduced a battery level in the `BeliefState` and simulated a low-battery condition by reducing the battery level to 2%.\n\nThe proposed coffee-delivery action was evaluated by the Warrant Gate. Although the proposal satisfied the grounded, feasible, safe, monitorable, repairable, and recordable criteria, the `energetically_viable` criterion returned `False`.\n\nThe Warrant Gate consequently rejected the action because the available battery energy was insufficient to execute the trajectory.\n\nThe system then redirected the workflow by generating a `RECHARGE_REQUESTED` action instead of proceeding with coffee delivery. After recharging, the battery level can be restored and the proposed action can be evaluated again.\n\nThis experiment demonstrates that energy availability is incorporated into cognitive decision-making and that the robot can select an appropriate recovery action when its operational resources are insufficient.\n\n### Task 5: Post-Hoc Epistemic Audit Analysis\nA core pillar of trustworthy robotics is the ability to audit *why* a decision was made or rejected.\n* **Question 5.1:** Write a post-processing function (`audit_analyzer(audit_trail)`) that parses the recorded episodic log. It should compute and print:\n  1. The total number of rejected proposals versus approved executions.\n  2. The most frequent warrant criterion responsible for rejection.\n  3. The average variance between predicted forces and observed physical outcomes.\n  \n\nI implemented the `audit_analyzer(audit_trail)` function to perform post-hoc analysis of the episodic audit trail.\n\nThe function computes the following metrics:\n\n1. **Rejected proposals:** Counts proposals rejected by the Warrant Gate.\n2. **Approved executions:** Counts approved actions that have an associated physical execution outcome.\n3. **Most frequent rejection criterion:** Identifies the warrant criterion that most frequently caused proposal rejection.\n4. **Average absolute force error:** Calculates the mean absolute difference between predicted contact force and actual measured contact force.\n\n### Experimental Results\n\n| Audit Metric | Observed Result |\n|---|---|\n| Total rejected proposals | 1 |\n| Total approved executions | 1 |\n| Most frequent rejection criterion | `safe` |\n| Average absolute force error | 0.020 N |\n\nThe results demonstrate that the audit analyzer can summarize the robot's decision-making process and quantify the difference between predicted and observed physical outcomes. This supports transparency, accountability, and post-hoc evaluation of cognitive robotic actions.\n* **Question 5.2:** Discuss in your lab report: *Why does maintaining separate records for `asked`, `belief`, `committed`, and `observed` solve the epistemological gap that pure data-driven imitation learning cannot address?*\n* Maintaining separate records for asked, belief, committed, and observed makes the robot's decision-making transparent and explainable.\n\nAsked: What the human requested.\n\nBelief: What the robot understands about the environment.\n\nCommitted: What action the robot decides to perform and whether it is approved.\n\nObserved: What actually happened during execution.\n\nUnlike pure imitation learning, which directly maps inputs to actions without explaining decisions, this approach helps us understand why an action was chosen, check whether it was safe, and compare predictions with actual results.\n\nThus, it improves transparency, explainability, and safety.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Part 3: Robot Anatomy (0.5 Pt)\n\n**Title:** Links Vs Joints, Actuators Vs Sensors, Effectors Vs Bases Vs Torsos Vs Heads, Wheels Vs Legs Vs Grippers, Proprioception Vs Exteroception Vs Interoception\n\n![Alternative Text](robot-body.jpg)\n\n### Questions\n\nIdentify the key functional parts of a robot based on the above picture and briefly indicate how they support the execution of function in the above architecture.\n\nA robot consists of several interconnected physical components that enable locomotion, manipulation, perception, and interaction with the environment.\n\n- **Links and joints:** Links provide structural support, while joints connect links and enable relative movement.\n- **Actuators and sensors:** Actuators generate motion, whereas sensors collect information about the robot and its environment.\n- **Effectors, base, torso, and head:** The effector performs manipulation, the base supports locomotion, the torso provides structural support, and the head typically houses sensors.\n- **Wheels, legs, and grippers:** Wheels and legs enable locomotion, while grippers are used to grasp and manipulate objects.\n- **Proprioception, exteroception, and interoception:** Proprioception senses the robot's own body configuration, exteroception senses the external environment, and interoception monitors its internal condition.\n\nTogether, these components support the execution of intelligent tasks. Sensors provide feedback to the perception system, while actuators and effectors execute the actions approved by the cognitive architecture.\n\n---","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Submission guidelines\n\n- Deadline: 03.11.2026, 4 PM\n- Submission as Git repo commits pushed within deadlines (Create a repo with the name **iis-assignment-win26** for the whole semester and invite me as collaborator)\n- Put all the answers in the notebook. Any other media related to your answer should be inserted in the notebook as well. The notebook and all related media should then be put into a folder called **Assignment1**. ","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"","metadata":{"trusted":false},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"","metadata":{"trusted":false},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null}]}
